# focus — a modus mentis that turns away from the clicked object observes another instead
#
# Ripelore is asked to focus on the test room door and turns away (--refuse-focus stands in for the
# choice judge answering "turn elsewhere", which --playground never does). It is not a dead end:
# Ripelore picks another unobserved object, and one text both dismisses the door and observes the
# new one — so the block carries a keyword, and the keyword names the new object, never the door.
# The follow-up observations run after it as they do after a kept focus.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Room" --period dawn --refuse-focus

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60
advance 12 180

# Observe the door with Ripelore; it turns to the weaver instead.
click keyword 0
choose 1
choose 0
advance 12 180
expect I am not interested in a test room door. My attention shifts to the weaver

# The turn carried the new object's keyword, wired to the weaver: observing through "man" focuses
# on the weaver (and, under --refuse-focus, turns away from it in turn).
click keyword man
choose 1
choose 1
advance 12 180
expect I am not interested in the weaver

quit
